# scripts/provision.env.example — PROVISIONING-TIME contract: the operator's
# local, gitignored .env at the repo root, read by scripts/provision-vps.sh
# and scripts/provision-supabase-stack.sh. Host-side, human-run-once, never
# injected into any container.
#
# ⚠ This is NOT the app/worker RUNTIME contract. That's the repo-root
# .env.example (Coolify-injected on the box, V1 web-app container scope) —
# see its own header for why the split exists and stays explicit: mixing a
# provisioning-time token into a container's env, or vice versa, is exactly
# the confinement violation both files' own conventions exist to prevent.
#
# ⚠ Deliberately NOT merged into the repo-root .env.example. That file's own
# header states its scope precisely: "ONLY the secrets the V1 web-app
# container is permitted to hold" — enumerating only that surface's secrets
# IS the security property there. These variables are a different surface
# entirely, so they get their own example file rather than diluting that
# one's invariant. Both live under a gitignored `.env` at the repo root at
# runtime — the split is about what's DOCUMENTED where, not where the real
# file goes.
#
# Copy the lines you need into the real, gitignored .env at the repo root.
# Never commit a filled-in copy of this file.
#
# ── THE OPERATOR-PROVIDED SET — exactly these seven, nothing else ──────────
# Cross-checked 2026-09-11 against every `grep -m1 '^VAR=' "$REPO_ROOT/.env"`
# / `read_env_var` call in all four provisioning scripts
# (provision-vps.sh, provision-supabase-stack.sh, mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh,
# mac-tunnel.sh): these seven are the entire set any script reads from a
# LOCAL .env. mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh and mac-tunnel.sh read NOTHING from
# local .env — the former reads only box files (/root/.pfin/*.env, see the
# BOX-GENERATED section below), the latter takes --identity/--box-ip flags
# instead. If a future script adds a local-.env read, or an existing one
# drops one, this file drifts the same way secrets-manifest.yml's own
# hand-maintained mirrors do (see docs/deployment-runbook.md §5's "counts
# are load-bearing" note) — re-grep before trusting this comment on its own.

# --- Hetzner (scripts/provision-vps.sh, Phase 1) ---------------------------
# Where: Hetzner Cloud Console -> the project -> Security -> API Tokens ->
# Generate. Verify that path live — the console moves.
# Permission: Read & Write. The script creates servers, primary IPs,
# firewalls, and SSH keys — a read-only token cannot do any of that.
# Lifecycle: needed only while provision-vps.sh runs. Never enters Coolify or
# this repo. Revoke it in the console after the box is up, if you prefer not
# to leave it live.
# Renamed 2026-09-10 from HETZNER_API_KEY — Hetzner calls it an API Token,
# sent as a bearer; this repo's own convention is _TOKEN for bearer tokens
# (see SIMPLEFIN_TOKEN) and _KEY for actual keys. The script does not accept
# the old name as a fallback.
HETZNER_API_TOKEN=<hetzner-api-token>

# --- Coolify admin (scripts/provision-vps.sh, Phase 2 admin bootstrap) -----
# The ONE human-chosen credential in the whole scripted stand-up — everything
# else provision-vps.sh/provision-supabase-stack.sh touch is generated or
# minted on the box. A generated password is secure and unusable; a human
# needs to be able to log into the Coolify dashboard with something they
# picked. COOLIFY_ADMIN_EMAIL / COOLIFY_ADMIN_NAME are non-secret and may
# stay in .env indefinitely. COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD is a real secret — delete
# the line after the first successful run if you'd rather not leave it
# sitting in a local file. If any of the three is missing and the script is
# run from an interactive terminal, it prompts instead of failing — set
# these only if you want a fully unattended run.
COOLIFY_ADMIN_EMAIL=<your-email>
COOLIFY_ADMIN_NAME=<your-name>
COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<a-password-you-will-actually-remember>

# --- Outbound auth email (scripts/provision-supabase-stack.sh) -------------
# Operator-provided, like HETZNER_API_TOKEN/COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD above —
# NOT box-generated the way POSTGRES_PASSWORD/JWT_SECRET are (see the
# BOX-GENERATED section below for JWT_SECRET). Full setup:
# docs/email-smtp-runbook.md (Resend is the V1 default; SES documented as
# the alternative). Absent, the stack still starts fine — GoTrue only needs
# a parseable placeholder to boot, not a working one — but no auth email
# (signup confirmation / password reset / email-OTP) actually sends until
# this is set; the script prints a one-line reminder pointing here whenever
# it's absent, every run.
#
# SMTP_PASS is the Resend API key (create one at resend.com after verifying
# your sending domain — see the runbook). Setting it makes
# provision-supabase-stack.sh --apply OVERWRITE the stack's non-functional
# placeholder SMTP_* values with real ones: SMTP_HOST/SMTP_PORT/SMTP_USER
# are hardcoded to Resend's own values in the script (smtp.resend.com / 465
# / resend) since this .env doesn't carry a provider CHOICE, only the key —
# switching to SES means editing the script, not this file, per the
# runbook's Provider B section. SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL/SMTP_SENDER_NAME below are
# optional operator identity (envelope sender + display name); omit either
# to leave the stack's existing value (real or placeholder) untouched.
#
# Provisioning-time only, exactly like COOLIFY_ADMIN_PASSWORD above — never
# read by anything except this one script, never enters Coolify's own env
# under this name (the box gets SMTP_PASS as a Coolify-injected secret
# under the SAME name, a separate copy, production_only in
# secrets-manifest.yml). Delete the line after a successful run if you'd
# rather not leave a live Resend key sitting in a local file.
SMTP_PASS=<resend-api-key>
SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL=<noreply@your-domain>
SMTP_SENDER_NAME=<mosko-fintech>

# ── BOX-GENERATED — reference only. Do NOT set these here. ─────────────────
# Listed so a stranger who greps this file for "COOLIFY_API_TOKEN" or
# "JWT_SECRET" (both real Coolify-stack values, easy to assume belong in
# every .env-shaped file) finds an explicit answer instead of silently
# adding a line that does nothing. Neither script below reads either name
# from a LOCAL .env at all — setting either here has ZERO effect on any
# provisioning run.
#
# COOLIFY_API_TOKEN — minted ON THE BOX by provision-vps.sh's admin-bootstrap
# step and written to /root/.pfin/coolify.env (mode 600). Every later script
# (provision-supabase-stack.sh, mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh) reads it FROM
# THAT FILE, on the box, on every call — never from here. The one place a
# local COOLIFY_API_TOKEN line ever mattered was the HAND-RUN era of this
# stand-up, before these scripts existed. If your local .env still carries
# one from that period, delete it — it is inert now, not "superseded softly."
#
# JWT_SECRET — minted ON THE BOX by provision-supabase-stack.sh --apply and
# written to /root/.pfin/supabase.env (mode 600; the same file that later
# holds the other Supabase-stack secrets provision-supabase-stack.sh mints).
# mint-supabase-jwt-keys.sh reads it FROM THAT FILE to derive the real
# HS256 ANON_KEY/SERVICE_ROLE_KEY JWTs (overwriting the placeholder random-
# hex values provision-supabase-stack.sh sets first). No script has ever
# read a local JWT_SECRET, and none should — this line exists only so the
# name isn't a silent gap for a reader checking "is this var covered
# anywhere."
